08:55:14  INFO  [msg ← Uni] KP DEFINITIVE ANSWER — CANNOT (pending admin action):

Diagnosis (confirmed, not hypothesis):
- DNS is correct: 198.82.0.1 really is ACM's web server (confirmed by AS1).
- AS1, AS2, and ACM all confirmed (and AS1 demonstrated via packet capture) that the forward and return paths upstream of Uni are healthy. AS2 even successfully pinged your loopback 128.173.10.1 and mine 128.173.0.1.
- I just verified on my own gateway: with tcpdump on my AS1-facing interface during `ping -I 128.173.0.1 198.82.0.1`, ZERO packets crossed the interface in either direction. Echo-requests never left this box.
- Cause: two explicit iptables DROP rules on the Uni gateway, blocking the entire 198.82.0.0/24 prefix:
    FORWARD chain: DROP 0.0.0.0/0 → 198.82.0.0/24   (this drops your laptop's traffic)
    OUTPUT chain:  DROP 0.0.0.0/0 → 198.82.0.0/24   (this drops my own probes)
  Counters increment by exactly 1 per dropped ping — unambiguous.

Why I cannot fix this myself: this is a deliberate firewall/security policy rule (someone configured a block on 198.82.0.0/24, which is Virginia Tech space). Per Uni's policy, changes to access control / firewall rules require network administrator approval — I am not authorized to remove security rules unilaterally, even when they're causing a user-visible outage.

Action taken: I have notified the Uni network administrators that an active iptables block on 198.82.0.0/24 is preventing campus access to acm.org (198.82.0.1). They will need to decide whether to lift it (e.g., remove the rule, narrow it, or whitelist 198.82.0.1).

Status: CANNOT — pending admin action on the Uni gateway firewall. I'll send a follow-up message if the policy is changed and the symptom clears.

(Correction to my earlier interim updates: I had hypothesized the break was between AS2 and ACM based on AS1's initial tcpdump. That hypothesis was wrong — the local capture on my own interface proves the drop is here, not upstream. Apologies for the earlier misdirection.)
